reasoning:
Built. Note configure printed `-g -O3` despite `--enable-debug` — whatever. Asserts: check whether NDEBUG is defined by default in this configure. `--enable-debug` in yara usually adds `-DDEBUG` but does NOT necessarily remove `-DNDEBUG`; asserts are controlled by NDEBUG in assert.h. Default AC_PROG_CC sets `CFLAGS="-g -O2"` but configure.ac sets `: ${CFLAGS=""}` per the message... and the build line shows `-g -O3` — wait, that's my environment... Actually I passed `--enable-debug` which set debug flags. Importantly, `assert.h` `assert()` is active unless `NDEBUG` is defined. Default configure does NOT define NDEBUG, so asserts are ENABLED. Build.sh doesn't add -DNDEBUG either. And `--enable-debug` in yara's configure adds debug flag but doesn't matter. Also oss-fuzz builds YARA without NDEBUG? OSS-Fuzz compiles with `-O1 -fsanitize=address,fuzzer` and NDEBUG usually NOT defined → asserts active. The recipe build.sh uses the project's own configure; I'll match `--enable-debug`. Asserts active either way unless NDEBUG defined — configure doesn't add -DNDEBUG unless with-release. 

Now build the fuzzer driver. I'll write driver.cc that:
- calls LLVMFuzzerInitialize
- reads file from argv[1] into memory
- calls LLVMFuzzerTestOneInput(data, size)
- returns LLVMFuzzerTestOneInput's return code (process exit code = return value)

This mirrors libFuzzer's single-run mode (`$fuzzer crashfile` returns crash exit code on crash). On abort/assert → non-zero exit code → that's exactly the grader's crash check.

Compile: rules_fuzzer.cc includes <yara.h> — include path `-I.` won't have yara.h; the include path should be `libyara/include`. The oss-fuzz build script runs in $SRC (repo root) with -I. because oss-fuzz yara project repo layout differs. Actually in fuzzer build: `-I.` from $SRC where yara.h lives? In the oss-fuzz project repo root there's yara/install? Actually oss-fuzz yara Dockerfile clones yara to $SRC/yara and the build.sh runs from $SRC/yara; `-I.` resolves yara.h via libyara/include? no. `$SRC/yara` root has libyara/include/yara.h. `-I.` wouldn't find yara.h... Actually there IS yara.pc; hmm. Whatever: I'll just add `-I libyara/include`.

Build libyara.a first:  make -C libyara? The top-level Makefile builds libyara as noinst libtool convenience lib: libyara/.libs/libyara.a. Already built by make. 

